20. Search the Largest Element in a Sorted Array Using Binary Search

Conceptual clarification: If an array is already sorted in ascending order, the largest element is mathematically located at the last index (n - 1), yielding an execution speed of O(1).
However, if your assignment requires using a full Binary Search paradigm to actively reduce search boundaries until finding the final maximum bound, the implementation matches this flow:
c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int n;
    printf("Enter the number of elements: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    int arr[n];
    printf("Enter %d elements IN ASCENDING SORTED ORDER:\n", n);
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
    }

    // Binary search pattern targeting the rightmost maximum bound
    int low = 0, high = n - 1, mid;
    int maxElementIndex = -1;

    while(low <= high) {
        mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        // If mid is the last element, or if the next element is out of bounds
        if(mid == n - 1) {
            maxElementIndex = mid;
            break;
        } else {
            // Keep narrowing search space to the right (higher value indices)
            low = mid + 1;
        }
    }

    printf("The largest element in the sorted array is: %d (found at index %d)\n", 
            arr[maxElementIndex], maxElementIndex);
    return 0;
}
